W: 1. Abroad as I was walking one evening in the spring
W: I heard a maid in Bedlam so sweetly for to sing.
W: Her chains she rattled in her hands and thus replied she:
W: “I love my love because I know my love loves me."
W:
W: 2. “Oh my cruel parents are being too unkind,
W: They drove and punish me and trouble my mind.
W: For though I’m ruined for his love contented will I be.
W: I love my love because I know my love loves me.
W:
W: 3. “Would I become a swallow ascend into the air,
W: And if I lost my lover and could not find him there.
W: I quickly would become a fish and search the flowing sea,
W: I love my love because I know my love loves me.
W:
W: 4. “With straw I’ll make a garland and dress it very fine,
W: I’ll mi the wame with roses lily pink and tyne.
W: I will preserve it for my love when he returns from sea,
W: I love my love because I know my love loves me.”
W:
W: 5. Just as she was sadly weeping her love came on the land,
W: Hearing she was in Bedlam he ran straight out of hand.
W: And as he entered into the gates he heard her sigh and say:
W: “I love my love because I know my love loves me.”
W:
W: 6. He stood and gazed on her hearing his love complain,
W: He could not stand any longer he bled in every vein.
W: He flew in to her snowy white arms and thus replied he:
W: “I love my love because I know my love loves me.”
